#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
"""onionhost-recipe: apply project-packaged app recipes (A23).

A recipe is a signed data package (onionhost-recipe-<id>) whose
payload lands in /usr/share/onionhost/recipes/<id>/: a manifest,
the pinned application archive (sha256 in the manifest), config
overlays, and seed material. This tool is the applier: it turns
the payload into a running site inside the slice 6B app slot. It
runs as root -

  * from onionhost-publish, when the owner's site model names a
    recipe (first install and any later re-publish), and
  * from the recipe package's own postinst, when the package is
    configured and the site uses that recipe AND the app is not
    installed yet.

Recipes are installers, not update channels (A26, owner decision
2026-10-21): applying a recipe installs the app; once an app is
installed on a machine, no update run ever applies a recipe over
it again - the installed tree is the owner's. The updater never
installs recipe packages, and the package postinst gates on the
state record this tool maintains (`is-installed` below): a
completed apply writes installed=True + the applied version, and
only that record makes an app "installed". A recipe's security
floor likewise gates install time only: a FIRST install whose
payload version is below the floor its own package declares is
refused here, before the site is touched; an installed app is
never measured against a floor.

Apply is careful in the same order the platform is:

  1. Verify the archive against the manifest's sha256. A payload
     that fails provenance never touches the site.
  2. Build the complete new www tree in a staging directory on
     the app-data volume: application files from the archive
     (installer/upgrade scripts NEVER land in the docroot),
     the app's data directories copied over from the live tree
     (uploads, avatars, cache survive every apply), and the
     app's config file - rendered fresh on first install with
     THIS machine's database credentials substituted in from
     /etc/onionhost/app-db.env (slice 6B flag 3: credentials are
     per-install, generated by onionhost-app-provision, and are
     never baked into the recipe package), carried over verbatim
     on later applies (it is the app's own state by then).
  3. On first install only, build the database seed: the app's
     own install SQL, substituted exactly the way its installer
     would (same replacement table, read from the payload), plus
     the recipe's seed tail (sealed-world settings + the forum
     administrator the OWNER chose at setup time - staged
     root-only by onionhost-wizard-helper recipe-setup and
     shredded here once consumed). The seed is staged at
     appdata/db-import.sql.gz, the slot's established import
     point: the app container's init imports it on first start
     and deletes it. An already-staged import (e.g. a restore's
     dump) is never overwritten.
  4. Write the recipe's overlays to their platform homes
     (/etc/onionhost/nginx-app-extra.conf,
     php-fpm-app-extra.conf, normalize-exceptions - the seams
     slice 6B left), then swap the staging tree into place with
     renames: the previous tree is kept one generation at
     appdata/.www-recipe-prev, so `onionhost-recipe rollback`
     can put it back. The two renames are not one atomic step,
     so apply first heals the crash window between them: a
     missing www beside a kept .www-recipe-prev is restored
     before anything else happens (S7).

Failure anywhere before the swap leaves the live site untouched.
Prints RECIPE_APPLY_OK / RECIPE_ROLLBACK_OK with details.

A recipe may instead seed itself by DRIVING the application's
own installer (manifest "install": an http-form hook) when the
application has no static install SQL to substitute - WordPress
is the case: its schema and default options live in PHP code, so
replicating them in SQL would bake a brittle per-version copy of
the installer into the recipe. For such recipes apply() only
lays the files and renders the config; after the container
starts, onionhost-publish runs `onionhost-recipe install-hook
<id>`, which speaks the installer's own form over the edge
socket with the owner's staged administrator details, verifies
the result, removes the installer files from the served tree,
marks the recipe seeded in its state record, and shreds the
staged credentials. The hook is idempotent: a site that already
answers "already installed" (a retry, or a restored machine
whose database came back with it) is finished, never re-seeded.
DokuWiki rides the same hook (its installer writes the admin
account with a native bcrypt hash - DokuWiki never upgrades a
legacy hash at login, so seeding one host-side, SMF-style, would
have left a permanently weaker hash; driving the installer makes
the question moot, as it did for WordPress).

Two more shapes complete the format (A23 recipes 3+4):

  * A recipe with NO database (manifest "db": {"required":
    false}) declares config_renderer "none" when its config is
    entirely the installer's / the archive's own work: apply
    then renders nothing and never demands database credentials.
    The platform side of db-less-ness (provisioning, the app
    init's MariaDB, backups) keys on the same manifest fact.
  * A NATIVE recipe (manifest "runtime": "native") ships the
    application as ONE file (manifest archive format "file" -
    the pinned binary itself, checksum-verified like any
    archive). Apply places it at appdata/native/app by atomic
    rename (the previous binary is kept one generation at
    .app-prev, the twin of .www-recipe-prev), renders the app's
    config from a payload template on first install only
    (manifest "native_config": per-install secrets are generated
    here, never packaged), and places recipe-managed files
    (manifest "native_files"). A native app that must create
    its administrator with its own CLI declares "first_boot":
    apply then stages a copy of the owner's administrator env
    on the app-data volume, the container's init runs the
    recipe's first-boot.sh before the app starts, and publish
    confirms completion through `onionhost-recipe
    await-first-boot <id>` (which shreds the host-side staged
    env once the volume's done-marker exists).

Manifest "extra_files" entries may set "after_data": true -
they are placed AFTER the data-directory carryover, so a
recipe-maintained file that lives inside a data directory (a
protected config the app's admin UI must not override) is
refreshed on every apply instead of being frozen at whatever
the live tree first held.
"""
import gzip
import hashlib
import http.client
import json
import os
import re
import secrets
import shutil
import socket
import struct
import sys
import tarfile
import time
import urllib.parse
import zipfile

PAYLOAD_ROOT = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_RECIPE_PAYLOAD_ROOT",
                              "/usr/share/onionhost/recipes")
ETC = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_ETC", "/etc/onionhost")
APPDATA = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_APPDATA",
                         "/var/lib/onionhost/appdata")
STATE_ROOT = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_RECIPE_STATE",
                            "/var/lib/onionhost/recipes")
HS_HOSTNAME = os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_HS_HOSTNAME", "/var/lib/onionhost/tor/hs/hostname")
ADMIN_ENV = os.path.join(ETC, "recipe-admin.env")
DB_ENV = os.path.join(ETC, "app-db.env")
EDGE_SOCK = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_EDGE_SOCK",
                           "/run/onionhost/web/b1.sock")

# The app container's fixed user-namespace map (the Quadlet
# unit's UIDMap/GIDMap 0:2300000:65536): container uid N is host
# uid 2300000 + N. Everything this tool stages onto the app-data
# volume must be owned by the MAPPED ids. A host-root file on the
# volume appears inside the container as "nobody" (its owner is
# outside the map): the application (www-data) cannot read it,
# and the container's own repair pass cannot fix it either -
# chowning a file whose owner is unmapped is refused inside a
# user namespace, which is why the app init's normalise pass
# silently leaves such files broken. Measured on the rig
# battery (2026-10-14, defects D1/D5/D6): recipe-seeded trees
# written as host root served HTTP 500 until they were chowned
# to exactly these ids. The base is env-overridable for the
# unit-test venues only (their nested uid space cannot carry
# the production map at all); production never sets it.
CONTAINER_UID_BASE = int(os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_CONTAINER_UID_BASE", "2300000"))
CONTAINER_ROOT_UID = CONTAINER_UID_BASE          # container root
CONTAINER_WWW_UID = CONTAINER_UID_BASE + 33      # www-data (33)
CONTAINER_WWW_GID = CONTAINER_UID_BASE + 33

# Files that must never reach the docroot: the application's own
# installer and upgrader are open doors once the site is live
# (APP-SITES.md says the same to hand installers). The seed SQL
# and the Settings template are consumed from the payload side,
# never served.
EXCLUDE_NAMES = {
    "other", "install.php", "upgrade.php", "upgrade-helper.php",
    "update_timezones.php", "update_unicode_data.php",
    "update_version_numbers.php", "composer.json", "composer.lock",
    "Settings.php", "Settings_bak.php", "db_last_error.php",
}
EXCLUDE_GLOBS = ("install_*.sql", "upgrade_*.sql")


def fail(msg):
    print("onionhost-recipe: %s" % msg, file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


def _read_env_file(path):
    out = {}
    with open(path) as f:
        for line in f:
            line = line.rstrip("\n")
            if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
                continue
            key, _, value = line.partition("=")
            out[key.strip()] = value
    return out


def _atomic_write(path, data, mode=0o644):
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    # A stale temp from an interrupted earlier write is removed
    # first: the temp is then created O_EXCL, BORN at its final
    # mode, so the data never sits at a wider mode between the
    # write and the rename (S5). os.open's mode is narrowed by
    # the umask, never widened, and fchmod on the still-empty
    # file pins the exact final mode before any bytes land.
    try:
        os.unlink(tmp)
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, mode)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
        os.fchmod(f.fileno(), mode)
        f.write(data)
        f.flush()
        os.fsync(f.fileno())
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def _check_recipe_relative_path(recipe_id, field, value):
    """Reject a recipe-supplied path that is not a plain
    relative path (Repo Review 4 task 11, B16): an absolute
    value or a ".." component would let a hostile or corrupted
    manifest read or place a file outside its intended
    directory when the value is joined onto the payload dir
    or the app-data volume."""
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value \
            or value.startswith("/") \
            or ".." in value.split("/"):
        raise ValueError(
            "recipe %s: %s %r is absolute or contains a '..' "
            "path component; refusing to use it as a path"
            % (recipe_id, field, value))


def _validate_manifest_paths(manifest, recipe_id):
    """Validate every recipe-supplied path the applier joins,
    at load time - before any join, verification read or
    placement, so a bad manifest fails before any write."""
    arc = manifest.get("archive")
    if isinstance(arc, dict) and "file" in arc:
        _check_recipe_relative_path(recipe_id, "archive.file",
                                    arc.get("file"))
    for item in manifest.get("native_files", []):
        if not isinstance(item, dict):
            raise ValueError(
                "recipe %s: native_files entry %r is not an "
                "object; refusing to use it"
                % (recipe_id, item))
        for field in ("src", "dst"):
            _check_recipe_relative_path(
                recipe_id, "native_files[].%s" % field,
                item.get(field))


def _load_manifest(recipe_id):
    path = os.path.join(PAYLOAD_ROOT, recipe_id, "manifest.json")
    with open(path) as f:
        manifest = json.load(f)
    if manifest.get("id") != recipe_id:
        raise ValueError("manifest id %r does not match %r"
                         % (manifest.get("id"), recipe_id))
    _validate_manifest_paths(manifest, recipe_id)
    return manifest


def _state_path(recipe_id):
    return os.path.join(STATE_ROOT, recipe_id, "state.json")


def _load_state(recipe_id):
    try:
        with open(_state_path(recipe_id)) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def _save_state(recipe_id, state):
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(_state_path(recipe_id)),
                exist_ok=True)
    _atomic_write(_state_path(recipe_id),
                  (json.dumps(state, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
                   + "\n").encode())
    # The version marker rides with the state record, never in
    # the served tree (deanonymization audit R-03): it used to be
    # written into the staging docroot, where the web server
    # served /RECIPE-VERSION.txt - the exact recipe and app
    # versions, on a predictable path - to anyone who asked.
    # Same one-line text, host-side home: next to state.json,
    # where only host tooling reads it. Every state writer goes
    # through here, so the marker always matches the record.
    marker = "%s %s (app %s)\n" % (state.get("id", recipe_id),
                                   state.get("version", ""),
                                   state.get("app_version", ""))
    _atomic_write(os.path.join(
        os.path.dirname(_state_path(recipe_id)),
        "RECIPE-VERSION.txt"), marker.encode())


def _app_installed(recipe_id):
    """A26's "installed": the state record carries a COMPLETED
    apply - installed=True plus the applied version. Every state
    writer that finishes an installation (the tree apply, the
    native apply, the install hook) writes exactly that shape;
    a first install that failed before completion leaves no
    such record, so the install path stays open for it. A
    rollback rewrites the same record with the restored
    version - the app is still installed."""
    state = _load_state(recipe_id)
    return bool(state.get("installed") and state.get("version"))


def _verrevcmp(a, b):
    """dpkg's verrevcmp (deb-version(7)): compare two upstream
    or revision strings - alternating non-digit / digit runs,
    '~' sorting before everything (including end-of-string),
    letters before non-letters."""
    i = j = 0
    la, lb = len(a), len(b)

    def order(c):
        if not c:
            return 0
        if c.isdigit():
            return 0
        if c.isalpha():
            return ord(c)
        if c == "~":
            return -1
        return ord(c) + 256

    while i < la or j < lb:
        first_diff = 0
        while (i < la and not a[i].isdigit()) or \
                (j < lb and not b[j].isdigit()):
            ac = order(a[i] if i < la else "")
            bc = order(b[j] if j < lb else "")
            if ac != bc:
                return -1 if ac < bc else 1
            if i < la and not a[i].isdigit():
                i += 1
            if j < lb and not b[j].isdigit():
                j += 1
        while i < la and a[i] == "0":
            i += 1
        while j < lb and b[j] == "0":
            j += 1
        while i < la and j < lb and a[i].isdigit() \
                and b[j].isdigit():
            if not first_diff:
                first_diff = ord(a[i]) - ord(b[j])
            i += 1
            j += 1
        if i < la and a[i].isdigit():
            return 1
        if j < lb and b[j].isdigit():
            return -1
        if first_diff:
            return 1 if first_diff > 0 else -1
    return 0


def _debver_cmp(a, b):
    """Debian version comparison in pure Python (epoch,
    upstream, revision). The applier cannot shell out to dpkg
    for this: it runs in minimal contexts where no dpkg binary
    exists (proven by the D8 proof machine), and the A26 floor
    check must work there too."""
    def parse(v):
        epoch = 0
        if ":" in v:
            head, v = v.split(":", 1)
            if head.isdigit():
                epoch = int(head)
        if "-" in v:
            up, rev = v.rsplit("-", 1)
        else:
            up, rev = v, ""
        return epoch, up, rev

    ea, ua, ra = parse(a)
    eb, ub, rb = parse(b)
    if ea != eb:
        return 1 if ea > eb else -1
    return _verrevcmp(ua, ub) or _verrevcmp(ra, rb)


def _check_install_floor(recipe_id, manifest, payload_dir):
    """A26: a recipe's security floor gates INSTALL TIME only.

    The payload carries the floor its package declares
    (floor.json beside the manifest, written by the package
    builder). A first install whose payload version is below
    that floor is refused before the app is placed: floors
    exist so a stale or replayed package cannot START an app,
    and after A26 the first install is the only moment they may
    judge a recipe at all - nothing here ever runs for an app
    that is already installed. Returns None when the install
    may proceed, else the refusal's exit code."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(payload_dir, "floor.json")) as f:
            floors = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    floor = floors.get("onionhost-recipe-" + recipe_id)
    if not floor:
        return None
    version = manifest["version"]
    if _debver_cmp(version, floor) < 0:
        return fail("recipe %s: payload version %s is below "
                    "this package's security floor %s - "
                    "refusing to install (floors gate install "
                    "time only, A26)" % (recipe_id, version,
                                         floor))
    return None


def _verify_archive(manifest, payload_dir):
    arc = manifest["archive"]
    path = os.path.join(payload_dir, "archive", arc["file"])
    digest = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        for chunk in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b""):
            digest.update(chunk)
    if digest.hexdigest() != arc["sha256"]:
        raise ValueError("archive sha256 mismatch: manifest %s, "
                         "file %s" % (arc["sha256"],
                                      digest.hexdigest()))
    return path


def _verify_native_files(manifest, payload_dir):
    """Verify every native_files entry against the sha256 the
    manifest pins for it, BEFORE anything is placed (S7).

    The archive itself is checksum-verified like this already;
    the recipe-managed files beside it (first-boot.sh,
    migrate.sh, the migrations, any helper binaries) were
    copied on trust in the package signature alone. An entry
    with NO sha256 in the manifest is a refusal, not a skip:
    the manifest is built from recipe.json, whose native_files
    entries all carry pins - a missing pin means the payload
    was not built the way the recipe declares."""
    for item in manifest.get("native_files", []):
        expected = item.get("sha256")
        if not expected:
            raise ValueError(
                "native file %s has no sha256 in the manifest; "
                "refusing to place unverified recipe files"
                % item.get("src"))
        path = os.path.join(payload_dir, item["src"])
        digest = hashlib.sha256()
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            for chunk in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b""):
                digest.update(chunk)
        if digest.hexdigest() != expected.lower():
            raise ValueError(
                "native file sha256 mismatch: %s manifest %s, "
                "file %s" % (item["src"], expected,
                             digest.hexdigest()))


def _restore_missing_www():
    """Recover an interrupted tree swap (S7): the swap renames
    the live tree to .www-recipe-prev, then the staging tree
    into its place - a crash between the two renames leaves NO
    docroot at all. A missing www beside a kept .www-recipe-prev
    can only be that window (a completed apply always leaves
    both), so the previous tree is put back before anything
    else happens: the site serves again immediately, even if
    this apply then fails (a bad new payload must not extend
    an outage a crash already started). Returns True when a
    tree was restored."""
    www = os.path.join(APPDATA, "www")
    prev = os.path.join(APPDATA, ".www-recipe-prev")
    if os.path.lexists(www) or not os.path.isdir(prev):
        return False
    os.rename(prev, www)
    print("onionhost-recipe: www was missing beside "
          ".www-recipe-prev (an interrupted swap); restored "
          "the previous tree")
    return True


def _extract_tree(archive_path, workdir, manifest):
    """Extract the app archive; return (tree_root, seed_sql_path,
    config_template_path). Both archive layouts are handled:
    the official zip (files at the top, installer files beside
    them) and the GitHub release zipball (one top folder, the
    installer machinery in its other/ subdirectory). The seed
    SQL and the config template are located by the names the
    manifest declares ("seed_sql", "config_template" - the SMF
    recipe's older key "settings_template" is honoured); a
    recipe with an install hook declares no seed SQL and gets
    None for it. Zip and tar archives are both supported
    (DokuWiki distributes a .tgz): the format is read off the
    archive file's name."""
    dest = os.path.join(workdir, "extract")
    lower = archive_path.lower()
    if lower.endswith(".zip"):
        with zipfile.ZipFile(archive_path) as z:
            z.extractall(dest)
    elif lower.endswith((".tar.gz", ".tgz", ".tar.xz", ".tar.bz2",
                         ".tar")):
        with tarfile.open(archive_path, "r:*") as t:
            t.extractall(dest, filter="data")
    else:
        raise ValueError("unsupported archive format: %s"
                         % archive_path)
    entries = [e for e in os.listdir(dest)
               if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(dest, e))]
    root = dest
    if len(entries) == 1 and os.path.isfile(
            os.path.join(dest, entries[0], "index.php")):
        root = os.path.join(dest, entries[0])
    seed_name = manifest.get("seed_sql")
    tpl_name = (manifest.get("config_template")
                or manifest.get("settings_template"))
    seed = settings = None
    for cand in (os.path.join(root, "other"),
                 root):
        if seed_name and seed is None:
            sql = os.path.join(cand, seed_name)
            if os.path.isfile(sql):
                seed = sql
        if tpl_name and settings is None:
            st = os.path.join(cand, tpl_name)
            if os.path.isfile(st):
                settings = st
    if seed_name and seed is None:
        raise ValueError("archive layout not recognised: no "
                         "install SQL %r found" % seed_name)
    if tpl_name and settings is None:
        raise ValueError("archive layout not recognised: no "
                         "config template %r found" % tpl_name)
    return root, seed, settings


def _chown_tree(root, uid, gid):
    """Give a staged tree to a container-namespace identity.

    Every file and directory under root (root itself included)
    is chowned to the mapped host ids; symlinks are chowned
    as links, never followed. Modes are untouched - the recipe
    mode exceptions and the app init's normalise pass still
    own those."""
    os.lchown(root, uid, gid)
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
        for name in dirnames + filenames:
            os.lchown(os.path.join(dirpath, name), uid, gid)


def _makedirs_owned(path, uid, gid):
    """makedirs, and give the directories this call actually
    creates to a container identity (see _chown_tree for why
    host-root ownership on the volume is broken). Pre-existing
    directories - the provisioned skeleton, the app's own data
    dirs - keep their owners."""
    missing = []
    probe = path
    while not os.path.isdir(probe):
        missing.append(probe)
        parent = os.path.dirname(probe)
        if parent == probe:
            break
        probe = parent
    os.makedirs(path, exist_ok=True)
    for d in missing:
        os.chown(d, uid, gid)


def _owner_gid():
    """The desktop owner's host group id, from the record
    onionhost-app-provision leaves in app-owner.env (the same
    record onionhost-generate-configs folds into the app
    container's environment). Falls back to the www-data group
    mapping when no record exists (an apply running before the
    first publish's provision, or a test venue)."""
    try:
        rec = _read_env_file(os.path.join(ETC, "app-owner.env"))
        return int(rec["ONIONHOST_OWNER_GID"])
    except (OSError, KeyError, ValueError):
        return CONTAINER_WWW_GID


def _owner_uid():
    """The desktop owner's host user id, from the same record
    as _owner_gid (None when no record exists - records
    written before the uid was included, or a test venue)."""
    try:
        rec = _read_env_file(os.path.join(ETC, "app-owner.env"))
        return int(rec["ONIONHOST_OWNER_UID"])
    except (OSError, KeyError, ValueError):
        return None


def _grant_owner_acl(path, is_dir=True):
    """Give the desktop owner rwx on a platform-managed app
    tree root via a named-user POSIX ACL (plus a default ACL
    on directories, so entries created later inherit it).

    Since the D8 fix, the app trees' group is the www-data
    group mapping - the owner's own group cannot serve,
    because a group outside the container's gid map makes
    the directory refuse every create through the gVisor
    stack (D7/D8). The ACL is the owner's replacement
    access path, the same grant onionhost-app-provision
    makes on the tree roots; it is re-asserted here so an
    apply or rollback leaves the whole state coherent.
    Written directly as the xattr wire format (no setfacl
    dependency); best-effort - provision re-asserts it at
    every publish regardless."""
    uid = _owner_uid()
    if uid is None:
        return

    def blob(owner_p, group_p, other_p):
        out = struct.pack("<I", 2)
        out += struct.pack("<HHI", 1, owner_p, 0xFFFFFFFF)
        out += struct.pack("<HHI", 2, 7, uid)
        out += struct.pack("<HHI", 4, group_p, 0xFFFFFFFF)
        out += struct.pack("<HHI", 16, group_p, 0xFFFFFFFF)
        out += struct.pack("<HHI", 32, other_p, 0xFFFFFFFF)
        return out
    try:
        mode = os.stat(path).st_mode
        data = blob((mode >> 6) & 7, (mode >> 3) & 7, mode & 7)
        os.setxattr(path, "system.posix_acl_access", data)
        if is_dir:
            os.setxattr(path, "system.posix_acl_default", data)
    except OSError:
        pass


def _own_native_root(native):
    """Give the native app root itself to the app user (D7),
    with a group the container's map carries (D8).

    The native root is the app process's working directory: the
    app (www-data) creates its data directory and files
    directly in it at runtime. Provision owns it to the www-data
    mapping from the start; this asserts the same state on
    every apply and rollback, so a root created or restored by
    anything else (an older provision, an older restore) is
    healed here rather than failing the app's first mkdir.
    The group is the www-data group mapping - NOT the owner's
    group, which is outside the container's gid map and made
    the root refuse every create (D8) - the mode is
    provision's setgid steady state, and the owner's desktop
    access is re-asserted as the named-user ACL."""
    os.chown(native, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
    os.chmod(native, 0o2775)
    _grant_owner_acl(native)


def _own_www_root(www):
    """Assert the app-writable state on the www root itself
    (D8). The root is a create path too (applications drop
    files beside their code), and the swap replaces it with
    the staging directory, whose mode is whatever the
    archive carried - without this assertion an apply
    silently demotes the root provision left at 02775 to
    the archive's 0755. Same doctrine as the tree roots:
    (www-data mapping, 02775) plus the owner's ACL."""
    os.chown(www, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
    os.chmod(www, 0o2775)
    _grant_owner_acl(www)


def _own_data_dirs(www, manifest):
    """Assert the app-writable state on a PHP recipe's
    declared data directories (D8).

    The directories a packaged application writes in at
    runtime (a forum's attachments and cache, a blog's
    uploads, a wiki's data tree) must satisfy the same rule
    as the native root: uid AND group inside the container's
    user-namespace map, or a create through the gVisor stack
    is refused. The tree-wide chown already gives them the
    www-data uid and group; what an older applier, a restore
    or the archive's own modes can leave behind is a missing
    setgid bit or a foreign group on the data directories
    and any subdirectories the application has since made.
    Every directory under each declared data directory is
    asserted to (www-data mapping, 02775) - the same
    directory doctrine the container init's normalise pass
    applies - and the owner's ACL is granted on the data
    directory roots."""
    for dirname in manifest.get("data_dirs", []):
        root = os.path.join(www, dirname)
        if not os.path.isdir(root):
            continue
        for dirpath, _dirnames, _filenames in os.walk(root):
            os.chown(dirpath, CONTAINER_WWW_UID,
                     CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
            os.chmod(dirpath, 0o2775)
        _grant_owner_acl(root)


def _shred_file(path):
    """Overwrite + unlink a staged secrets file (the recipe
    administrator env): its one job is done, and the plaintext
    password must not linger on the host."""
    try:
        with open(path, "r+b") as f:
            f.write(b"\x00" * os.fstat(f.fileno()).st_size)
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.unlink(path)
    except OSError as e:
        # A failed shred must not pass silently (S6): the whole
        # point of this file is that the plaintext does not
        # linger, so a copy that could not be destroyed is
        # exactly the fact the operator needs in the log.
        print("onionhost-recipe: WARNING: could not shred %s: %s"
              % (path, e), file=sys.stderr)


def _php_single_unquote(raw):
    """Undo PHP single-quote escaping (only \\\\ and \\' are
    escapes in PHP single-quoted strings)."""
    out = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(raw):
        ch = raw[i]
        if ch == "\\" and i + 1 < len(raw) and raw[i + 1] in "\\'":
            out.append(raw[i + 1])
            i += 2
        else:
            out.append(ch)
            i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def _sql_escape(value):
    """mysqli_real_escape_string semantics (SMF's
    db_escape_string for the mysql driver)."""
    return (value.replace("\\", "\\\\")
                 .replace("\x00", "\\0")
                 .replace("\n", "\\n")
                 .replace("\r", "\\r")
                 .replace("'", "\\'")
                 .replace('"', '\\"')
                 .replace("\x1a", "\\Z"))


def _php_escape(value):
    return value.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("'", "\\'")


def _load_language_defaults(tree_root):
    """The installer's {$default_*} replacements come from its
    language file ($txt keys starting with default_). Read them
    from the payload's own copy so the seed says exactly what a
    hand install in this language would say."""
    path = os.path.join(tree_root, "Themes", "default",
                        "languages", "Install.english.php")
    defaults = {}
    pattern = re.compile(
        r"\$txt\['(default_[^']+)'\]\s*=\s*'((?:[^'\\]|\\.)*)'\s*;")
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
        for match in pattern.finditer(f.read()):
            defaults[match.group(1)] = _php_single_unquote(
                match.group(2))
    if len(defaults) < 20:
        raise ValueError("language defaults not found in %s" % path)
    return defaults


def build_seed_sql(manifest, seed_path, tree_root, onion,
                   admin):
    """Reproduce SMF's installer SQL step: the app's install SQL
    with the installer's own replacement table (other/install.php
    DatabasePopulation), plus the recipe's seed tail."""
    with open(seed_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        sql = f.read()
    prefix = manifest["db"]["prefix"]
    boarddir = "/srv/appdata/www"
    boardurl = "http://" + onion
    replaces = {
        "{$db_prefix}": prefix,
        "{$boarddir}": _sql_escape(boarddir),
        "{$boardurl}": _sql_escape(boardurl),
        "{$attachdir}": _sql_escape(json.dumps(
            {"1": boarddir + "/attachments"})),
        "{$engine}": "InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8 "
                     "COLLATE=utf8_general_ci",
        "{$memory}": "InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8 "
                     "COLLATE=utf8_general_ci",
        "{$smf_version}": manifest["app"]["version"],
        "{$registration_method}": "0",
        "{$sched_task_offset}": str(82800 + secrets.randbelow(86400)),
        # The installer's two checkbox settings, both checked by
        # default in its form: gzip output on, database-driven
        # sessions on - a default hand install's values.
        "{$enableCompressedOutput}": "1",
        "{$databaseSession_enable}": "1",
    }
    for key, value in _load_language_defaults(tree_root).items():
        replaces["{$%s}" % key] = _sql_escape(value)
    # The installer's own special case: in default_reserved_names
    # the escaped backslash-n sequences are collapsed back so the
    # stored value carries real newlines.
    key = "{$default_reserved_names}"
    if key in replaces:
        replaces[key] = replaces[key].replace("\\\\n", "\\n")
    pattern = re.compile("|".join(
        re.escape(k) for k in sorted(replaces, key=len,
                                     reverse=True)))
    sql = pattern.sub(lambda m: replaces[m.group(0)], sql)
    leftovers = sorted(set(re.findall(r"\{\$[A-Za-z0-9_]+\}", sql)))
    if leftovers:
        raise ValueError("unsubstituted placeholders in seed SQL: "
                         + ", ".join(leftovers))

    tail_path = os.path.join(PAYLOAD_ROOT, manifest["id"],
                             "seed", "tail.sql.tmpl")
    with open(tail_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        tail = f.read()
    user_sql = _sql_escape(admin["user"])
    tail = (tail.replace("__PREFIX__", prefix)
                .replace("__ADMIN_USER__", user_sql)
                .replace("__ADMIN_EMAIL__",
                         _sql_escape(admin["email"]))
                .replace("__ADMIN_PASS_SHA1__", admin["pass_sha1"])
                .replace("__ADMIN_SALT__", admin["salt"])
                .replace("__NOW__", str(int(time.time()))))
    return sql + "\n" + tail


def render_settings(template_path, onion, db):
    """Render the app's Settings.php from the payload template
    with this machine's values: the database credentials from
    /etc/onionhost/app-db.env (flag 3 - per-install, never baked
    into the recipe), the site's onion address, and the sealed-
    world switches that live in this file (image proxy off)."""
    with open(template_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    subs = [
        (r"^\$boardurl = '[^']*';",
         "$boardurl = '%s';" % _php_escape("http://" + onion)),
        (r"^\$webmaster_email = '[^']*';",
         "$webmaster_email = '%s';"
         % _php_escape("admin@" + onion)),
        (r"^\$db_name = '[^']*';",
         "$db_name = '%s';" % _php_escape(db["DB_NAME"])),
        (r"^\$db_user = '[^']*';",
         "$db_user = '%s';" % _php_escape(db["DB_USER"])),
        (r"^\$db_passwd = '[^']*';",
         "$db_passwd = '%s';" % _php_escape(db["DB_PASSWORD"])),
        (r"^\$image_proxy_enabled = true;",
         "$image_proxy_enabled = false;"),
        (r"^\$image_proxy_secret = '[^']*';",
         "$image_proxy_secret = '%s';" % secrets.token_hex(12)),
    ]
    for pattern, repl in subs:
        text, n = re.subn(pattern, repl.replace("\\", "\\\\"),
                          text, count=1, flags=re.M)
        if n != 1:
            raise ValueError("Settings.php template: pattern not "
                             "found: %s" % pattern)
    return text


# Salts are single-quoted into the rendered config, so the
# alphabet excludes the quote and the backslash (the only two
# escapes PHP single-quoted strings honour); 64 chars each from
# this host's CSPRNG, per install, never in the recipe package.
_WP_SALT_ALPHABET = (
    "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ"
    "0123456789!@#$%^&*()-_=+[]{};:,.<>?/~")


def _wp_salt():
    return "".join(secrets.choice(_WP_SALT_ALPHABET)
                   for _ in range(64))


def render_wp_config(template_path, db, manifest):
    """Render wp-config.php from the archive's own sample with
    this machine's values: the database credentials from
    /etc/onionhost/app-db.env (per-install, never baked into the
    recipe), the manifest's table prefix, eight freshly
    generated authentication salts, and the sealed-world
    constants (outbound HTTP blocked at the WordPress API level,
    WordPress's own updater disabled - it could only ever work
    by downloading, which the sealed room forbids; updates are
    the owner's, by file replacement (A26) - WP-Cron disabled
    for the same reason, and the theme/plugin code editor off,
    so an administrator session cannot become code execution)."""
    with open(template_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    for token, key in (("database_name_here", "DB_NAME"),
                       ("username_here", "DB_USER"),
                       ("password_here", "DB_PASSWORD")):
        needle = "'%s'" % token
        if text.count(needle) != 1:
            raise ValueError("wp-config sample: %s not found "
                             "exactly once" % needle)
        text = text.replace(needle,
                            "'%s'" % _php_escape(db[key]))
    if text.count("put your unique phrase here") != 8:
        raise ValueError("wp-config sample: expected 8 salt "
                         "placeholders")
    for _ in range(8):
        text = text.replace("put your unique phrase here",
                            _wp_salt(), 1)
    prefix = manifest["db"]["prefix"]
    text, n = re.subn(r"^\$table_prefix = '[^']*';",
                      "$table_prefix = '%s';"
                      % _php_escape(prefix),
                      text, count=1, flags=re.M)
    if n != 1:
        raise ValueError("wp-config sample: $table_prefix line "
                         "not found")
    marker = "/* Add any custom values between this line"
    idx = text.find(marker)
    if idx < 0:
        raise ValueError("wp-config sample: custom-values "
                         "marker not found")
    line_end = text.find("\n", idx)
    defines = (
        "\n// ** OnionHost sealed-world settings - packaged "
        "recipe (A23). ** //\n"
        "// No outbound HTTP from WordPress core, plugins or\n"
        "// themes: this site has no network of its own, and a\n"
        "// request that cannot be answered locally fails at\n"
        "// once instead of dialling out (and being blocked at\n"
        "// the container wall anyway).\n"
        "define( 'WP_HTTP_BLOCK_EXTERNAL', true );\n"
        "// WordPress's own updater is disabled: it works by\n"
        "// downloading, and this site has no network to\n"
        "// download with. Updates are the owner's, made by\n"
        "// replacing files in the app folder (Owner Guide\n"
        "// section 4) - the platform installed WordPress once\n"
        "// and never changes it again (A26).\n"
        "define( 'AUTOMATIC_UPDATER_DISABLED', true );\n"
        "define( 'WP_AUTO_UPDATE_CORE', false );\n"
        "// WP-Cron is disabled (deanonymization audit R-21):\n"
        "// with the updater off and outbound HTTP blocked,\n"
        "// cron's remaining jobs here are version-check and\n"
        "// housekeeping noise whose loopback self-requests\n"
        "// achieve nothing the platform does not already do\n"
        "// on its own schedule. Scheduled posts are the one\n"
        "// visible cost - WordPress's own answer, an external\n"
        "// cron trigger, has no sealed-room equivalent; the\n"
        "// trade is recorded in the build log's audit trail.\n"
        "define( 'DISABLE_WP_CRON', true );\n"
        "// The theme/plugin code editor is off: an\n"
        "// administrator session must not become code\n"
        "// execution. Files are managed in the app folder.\n"
        "define( 'DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true );\n")
    return text[:line_end + 1] + defines + text[line_end + 1:]


def _copy_tree(src, dst, tree_root=None,
               exclude_rels=frozenset()):
    """Copy a tree, skipping __pycache__ anywhere and any path
    in exclude_rels (paths relative to tree_root - the manifest's
    exclude_paths / install_files mechanism)."""
    def ignore(dirpath, names):
        out = []
        for name in names:
            if name == "__pycache__":
                out.append(name)
            elif tree_root is not None and os.path.relpath(
                    os.path.join(dirpath, name),
                    tree_root) in exclude_rels:
                out.append(name)
        return out
    shutil.copytree(src, dst, symlinks=False, ignore=ignore)


def _excluded(name):
    if name in EXCLUDE_NAMES:
        return True
    return any(re.fullmatch(g.replace("*", ".*"), name)
               for g in EXCLUDE_GLOBS)


def _staged_admin(recipe_id):
    """The owner's staged administrator details (written
    root-only by onionhost-wizard-helper recipe-setup), validated
    for THIS recipe; returns {user, password, email, title}.
    Raises ValueError with a plain message when they are
    missing, incomplete or staged for another recipe - a first
    install without the owner's own choice is never honest, so
    every caller fails BEFORE touching the site."""
    if not os.path.isfile(ADMIN_ENV):
        raise ValueError(
            "first install of recipe %s needs the administrator "
            "details (staged by onionhost-wizard-helper "
            "recipe-setup); nothing was changed" % recipe_id)
    env = _read_env_file(ADMIN_ENV)
    if env.get("RECIPE_ID") != recipe_id:
        raise ValueError(
            "staged administrator details are for recipe %r, "
            "not %r; nothing was changed"
            % (env.get("RECIPE_ID"), recipe_id))
    user = env.get("RECIPE_ADMIN_USER", "").strip()
    password = env.get("RECIPE_ADMIN_PASSWORD", "")
    if not user or not password:
        raise ValueError("staged administrator details are "
                         "incomplete; nothing was changed")
    try:
        onion = open(HS_HOSTNAME).read().strip()
    except OSError:
        onion = ""
    return {
        "user": user,
        "password": password,
        "email": env.get("RECIPE_ADMIN_EMAIL")
                 or ("admin@" + onion if onion else
                     "admin@localhost.invalid"),
        "title": env.get("RECIPE_SITE_TITLE", ""),
    }


def _write_overlays(recipe_id):
    """Write the recipe's overlay files to their platform homes
    in /etc/onionhost (the seams slice 6B left: the nginx and
    PHP-FPM fragments the golden configs include, and the
    permission-normaliser's exception list)."""
    overlay_dir = os.path.join(PAYLOAD_ROOT, recipe_id, "overlay")
    for name in ("nginx-app-extra.conf", "php-fpm-app-extra.conf",
                 "normalize-exceptions"):
        src = os.path.join(overlay_dir, name)
        if os.path.isfile(src):
            with open(src, "rb") as f:
                _atomic_write(os.path.join(ETC, name), f.read())


def _apply_mode_exceptions(recipe_id):
    """Enforce the recipe's permission-normaliser exception
    modes (payload overlay/normalize-exceptions: `MODE PATH`,
    PATH relative to the app-data volume) on files that exist
    NOW. The app container's init applies the same list at every
    container start; running it here too closes the one window
    the init cannot reach: files an install hook creates DURING
    first boot (an installer's users file holding password
    hashes, written after the init's pass has already run).
    Entries are validated exactly as app-init validates them."""
    path = os.path.join(PAYLOAD_ROOT, recipe_id, "overlay",
                        "normalize-exceptions")
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            lines = f.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return
    for line in lines:
        parts = line.split(None, 1)
        if len(parts) != 2:
            continue
        mode, rel = parts
        if rel.startswith("#") or rel.startswith("/") \
                or ".." in rel.split("/"):
            continue
        try:
            mode_int = int(mode, 8)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        target = os.path.join(APPDATA, rel)
        if os.path.isfile(target):
            os.chmod(target, mode_int)


# The native slot's first-boot conventions (A23 recipes 3+4):
# fixed platform names, because the app container's init must
# find them without reading the recipe manifest (which is not
# mounted into the container). A native recipe with a first-boot
# step ships first-boot.sh (via native_files); the applier
# stages the owner's administrator env at FIRST_BOOT_ENV on the
# volume; the step consumes + shreds that copy and leaves
# FIRST_BOOT_DONE behind.
FIRST_BOOT_ENV = ".recipe-admin.env"
FIRST_BOOT_DONE = ".first-boot-done"


def render_native_config(template_path, manifest, title):
    """Render a native recipe's config template (manifest
    "native_config") with this machine's values: the onion
    address, the site title, and per-install secrets generated
    here from the host CSPRNG - they are never in the recipe
    package (the same rule the PHP config renderers keep)."""
    with open(template_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    try:
        onion = open(HS_HOSTNAME).read().strip()
    except OSError:
        onion = ""
    if not onion:
        raise ValueError("no onion address yet (%s); cannot "
                         "render the app config" % HS_HOSTNAME)
    subs = {
        "__ONION__": onion,
        "__SITE_TITLE__": title or manifest["app"]["name"],
        "__SECRET_KEY__": secrets.token_hex(32),
        "__INTERNAL_TOKEN__": secrets.token_urlsafe(48),
    }
    for token, value in subs.items():
        text = text.replace(token, value)
    leftovers = sorted(set(re.findall(r"__[A-Z0-9_]+__", text)))
    if leftovers:
        raise ValueError("native config template: unsubstituted "
                         "tokens: " + ", ".join(leftovers))
    return text


def apply_native(recipe_id, manifest, payload_dir, state,
                 archive_path):
    """Apply a native recipe: the application is ONE file (the
    pinned binary, checksum-verified by the caller), placed on
    the app-data volume's native tree beside a rendered config
    and recipe-managed files. There is no docroot swap: the
    binary is replaced by atomic rename (a running process keeps
    the old inode; the updater's restart picks the new binary
    up), the previous binary is kept one generation at
    .app-prev, and the config - the app's own state once
    written - is rendered on first install only, judged by the
    config file itself (a restored machine carries it, plus its
    data, but no recipe state - and must sync, never re-seed)."""
    if manifest.get("archive", {}).get("format") != "file":
        return fail("native recipe %s: the archive must be the "
                    "application file itself (manifest archive "
                    "format \"file\")" % recipe_id)
    # Provenance for the recipe-managed files, checked before
    # ANY of them (or anything else) is placed (S7, task 14).
    _verify_native_files(manifest, payload_dir)
    version = manifest["version"]
    native = os.path.join(APPDATA, "native")
    _makedirs_owned(native, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID)
    # The root itself belongs to the app user, not the
    # container root (D7 - see _own_native_root).
    _own_native_root(native)
    cfg_spec = manifest.get("native_config")
    cfg_dst = None
    first_install = True
    if cfg_spec:
        cfg_dst = os.path.join(native, cfg_spec["dst"])
        first_install = not os.path.isfile(cfg_dst)
    staged = None
    if first_install and manifest.get("first_boot"):
        # Fail BEFORE changing anything when the owner's
        # administrator details are not staged.
        staged = _staged_admin(recipe_id)
    if first_install:
        # A26: the package's floor gates this install, and only
        # this install (see _check_install_floor).
        refused = _check_install_floor(recipe_id, manifest,
                                       payload_dir)
        if refused is not None:
            return refused
    # Recipe-managed files (the first-boot step, any helpers):
    # refreshed from the payload on every apply. They are read
    # and run by the container's init, so they belong to the
    # container's root mapping (D5/D6: staged as host root they
    # would be "nobody" in the namespace and unreadable where
    # their modes are tight).
    for item in manifest.get("native_files", []):
        src = os.path.join(payload_dir, item["src"])
        dst = os.path.join(native, item["dst"])
        _makedirs_owned(os.path.dirname(dst),
                        CONTAINER_ROOT_UID, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID)
        shutil.copy2(src, dst)
        if item.get("mode"):
            os.chmod(dst, int(item["mode"], 8))
        os.chown(dst, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID)
    if first_install and cfg_spec:
        title = (staged or {}).get("title") \
            or manifest.get("default_title", "")
        rendered = render_native_config(
            os.path.join(payload_dir, cfg_spec["src"]),
            manifest, title)
        _makedirs_owned(os.path.dirname(cfg_dst),
                        CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
        _atomic_write(cfg_dst, rendered.encode(), mode=0o640)
        # The app process (www-data) is the config's reader;
        # owned by host root it is unreadable in the namespace
        # at any tight mode (D6 - the app died at startup).
        os.chown(cfg_dst, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
    if first_install and manifest.get("first_boot"):
        # Stage the administrator env ON THE VOLUME: the app
        # container's init hands it to the recipe's first-boot
        # step, which consumes and shreds this copy; the host
        # copy is shredded by await-first-boot once the step
        # reports done. Same 0400 discipline as the host file -
        # and the volume copy belongs to the container's root,
        # the only identity that may read it there (D5: as host
        # root 0400 it was "nobody"-owned in the namespace and
        # the first-boot step got Permission denied, so the
        # administrator was never created).
        with open(ADMIN_ENV, "rb") as f:
            blob = f.read()
        env_dst = os.path.join(native, FIRST_BOOT_ENV)
        _atomic_write(env_dst, blob, mode=0o400)
        os.chown(env_dst, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID, CONTAINER_ROOT_UID)
    _write_overlays(recipe_id)
    # The binary itself: stage beside the live one, then rename.
    tmp = os.path.join(native, ".app.new")
    with open(archive_path, "rb") as fin, \
            open(tmp, "wb") as fout:
        shutil.copyfileobj(fin, fout)
        fout.flush()
        os.fsync(fout.fileno())
    os.chmod(tmp, 0o755)
    # The app runs as www-data; the binary is staged under its
    # mapping, like the rest of the native tree's working files.
    os.chown(tmp, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
    app = os.path.join(native, "app")
    prev = os.path.join(native, ".app-prev")
    if os.path.isfile(app):
        os.replace(app, prev)
    os.replace(tmp, app)
    _apply_mode_exceptions(recipe_id)
    _save_state(recipe_id, {
        "id": recipe_id,
        "version": version,
        "app_version": manifest["app"]["version"],
        "installed": True,
        "seeded": (not manifest.get("first_boot"))
                  or os.path.exists(os.path.join(
                      native, FIRST_BOOT_DONE)),
        "prev_version": state.get("version"),
        "applied_at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ",
                                    time.gmtime()),
    })
    print("RECIPE_APPLY_OK id=%s version=%s runtime=native "
          "first_install=%s" % (recipe_id, version,
                                "yes" if first_install else "no"))
    return 0


def await_first_boot(recipe_id):
    """Publish-side completion check for a native recipe's
    first-boot step: wait for the done marker the step leaves
    on the app-data volume, then shred the host-side staged
    administrator env (the volume copy was shredded by the step
    itself). A timeout fails the publish - the site may be up,
    but its administrator does not exist yet, and re-publishing
    retries the wait (the step is marker-gated and idempotent).
    Recipes without a first-boot step no-op, exactly like the
    install hook."""
    manifest = _load_manifest(recipe_id)
    if not manifest.get("first_boot"):
        return 0
    done = os.path.join(APPDATA, "native", FIRST_BOOT_DONE)
    timeout = float(os.environ.get(
        "ONIONHOST_FIRST_BOOT_TIMEOUT", "240"))
    deadline = time.time() + timeout
    while time.time() < deadline:
        if os.path.exists(done):
            _shred_file(ADMIN_ENV)
            print("FIRST_BOOT_OK id=%s" % recipe_id)
            return 0
        time.sleep(2)
    return fail("first-boot step for recipe %s did not report "
                "done; the site is up but its administrator "
                "may not exist yet - publish again to retry"
                % recipe_id)


def apply_recipe(recipe_id):
    payload_dir = os.path.join(PAYLOAD_ROOT, recipe_id)
    manifest = _load_manifest(recipe_id)
    state = _load_state(recipe_id)
    version = manifest["version"]
    if not os.path.isdir(APPDATA):
        return fail("app slot not provisioned (%s missing)"
                    % APPDATA)
    if manifest.get("runtime") != "native":
        # Heal an interrupted earlier swap first (S7): publish
        # reaches the site through this apply, so this is the
        # point where a missing docroot becomes a serving site
        # again - before the new payload is even verified.
        _restore_missing_www()
    archive_path = _verify_archive(manifest, payload_dir)
    if manifest.get("runtime") == "native":
        return apply_native(recipe_id, manifest, payload_dir,
                            state, archive_path)

    workdir = os.path.join(STATE_ROOT, recipe_id, "work")
    shutil.rmtree(workdir, ignore_errors=True)
    os.makedirs(workdir)
    tree_root, seed_sql_path, settings_tpl = _extract_tree(
        archive_path, workdir, manifest)

    www = os.path.join(APPDATA, "www")
    config_files = manifest.get("config_carryover") or [
        "Settings.php", "Settings_bak.php"]
    config_file = manifest.get("config_file") or "Settings.php"
    live_config = os.path.join(www, config_file)
    if "install" in manifest:
        # Installer-driven recipe: the app's ground truth (its
        # database) is not readable from the host, so "already
        # installed" is the state record the install hook
        # maintains (seeded=True once the installer has run). A
        # backup restored onto a fresh machine carries no recipe
        # state, so apply treats it as a first install - and the
        # hook's probe then finds the restored database already
        # installed and finishes without re-seeding; the config
        # itself is carried over below, because it exists in the
        # restored tree.
        first_install = not state.get("seeded")
    else:
        # First install is judged by the app's own config file,
        # NOT by this tool's state record: a restored machine
        # carries a complete www (the config file included) but
        # no recipe state - the state file is per-machine
        # bookkeeping and is not part of a backup - and it must
        # sync, never re-seed. The config file is the ground
        # truth that the app is already installed.
        first_install = not os.path.isfile(live_config)

    if first_install:
        # A26: the package's floor gates this install, and only
        # this install - an installed app never reaches this
        # check because it is never a first install again.
        refused = _check_install_floor(recipe_id, manifest,
                                       payload_dir)
        if refused is not None:
            return refused

    admin = None
    if first_install and seed_sql_path is not None:
        # The administrator is the owner's own choice, staged
        # root-only by the wizard helper. Without it there is no
        # honest first install - fail BEFORE touching the site.
        staged = _staged_admin(recipe_id)
        admin = {
            "user": staged["user"],
            "email": staged["email"],
            # SMF legacy login hash (see the seed tail for why):
            # sha1 of lowercased member name + password, exactly
            # the form SMF 2.1 accepts and upgrades to bcrypt at
            # first login.
            "pass_sha1": hashlib.sha1(
                (staged["user"].lower()
                 + staged["password"]).encode("utf-8")
                ).hexdigest(),
            "salt": secrets.token_hex(16),
        }

    try:
        onion = open(HS_HOSTNAME).read().strip()
    except OSError:
        onion = ""

    # ---- build the staging tree -------------------------------
    staging = os.path.join(APPDATA, ".recipe-stage")
    shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
    os.makedirs(staging)
    exclude_rels = set(manifest.get("exclude_paths", []))
    install_files = set(manifest.get("install_files", []))
    if not first_install:
        # The app's installer/upgrader files had their one
        # moment during the install hook; they never persist in
        # a live tree.
        exclude_rels |= install_files
    for name in sorted(os.listdir(tree_root)):
        if _excluded(name) and not (
                first_install and name in install_files):
            # The global name filter keeps installer files out
            # of every docroot - except a hook recipe's DECLARED
            # installer on its first install, which the hook
            # needs in the tree (WordPress keeps its installer
            # nested, DokuWiki's is top-level) and removes the
            # moment installation verifies.
            continue
        if name in exclude_rels:
            continue
        src = os.path.join(tree_root, name)
        dst = os.path.join(staging, name)
        if os.path.isdir(src):
            _copy_tree(src, dst, tree_root=tree_root,
                       exclude_rels=exclude_rels)
        else:
            shutil.copy2(src, dst)
    # Extra payload files: platform files the recipe places into
    # the tree (a must-use plugin, say), refreshed on every
    # apply. Entries marked "after_data" are placed later -
    # after the data-directory carryover - instead: they live
    # inside a data directory but are recipe-maintained, so the
    # live copy must not freeze them (a protected config the
    # app's admin UI cannot override, say).
    extras = manifest.get("extra_files", [])
    for item in extras:
        if item.get("after_data"):
            continue
        src = os.path.join(payload_dir, item["src"])
        dst = os.path.join(staging, item["dst"])
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dst), exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(src, dst)
    # Data directories: the live tree's copies win (they hold the
    # owner's uploads/avatars); on first install the archive's
    # empty guarded directories are already in place.
    for dirname in manifest.get("data_dirs", []):
        live = os.path.join(www, dirname)
        staged = os.path.join(staging, dirname)
        if os.path.isdir(live):
            shutil.rmtree(staged, ignore_errors=True)
            _copy_tree(live, staged)
        elif not os.path.isdir(staged):
            # The app's distribution ships no such directory
            # (WordPress's uploads/, which it would otherwise
            # create lazily at runtime); the recipe declares it
            # a data directory, so it exists from the start.
            os.makedirs(staged, exist_ok=True)
    # The app config: carried over once the app owns it; rendered
    # from the template (with this machine's DB credentials) on
    # first install. A recipe with config_renderer "none" has no
    # host-rendered config at all (its installer writes the
    # config, or the archive's defaults rule): nothing is
    # rendered and no database credentials are demanded - the
    # shape a database-less recipe (DokuWiki) takes.
    renderer = manifest.get("config_renderer", "smf-settings")
    if os.path.isfile(live_config):
        for name in config_files:
            src = os.path.join(www, name)
            if os.path.isfile(src):
                shutil.copy2(src, os.path.join(staging, name))
    elif renderer == "none":
        pass
    else:
        db = _read_env_file(DB_ENV)
        for key in ("DB_NAME", "DB_USER", "DB_PASSWORD"):
            if key not in db:
                return fail("database credentials incomplete in "
                            "%s" % DB_ENV)
        if renderer == "wp-config":
            rendered = render_wp_config(settings_tpl, db, manifest)
            with open(os.path.join(staging, config_file),
                      "w") as f:
                f.write(rendered)
        else:
            if not onion:
                return fail("no onion address yet (%s); cannot "
                            "render the app config" % HS_HOSTNAME)
            rendered = render_settings(settings_tpl, onion, db)
            for name in ("Settings.php", "Settings_bak.php"):
                with open(os.path.join(staging, name), "w") as f:
                    f.write(rendered)
    # Late extra files ("after_data"): recipe-maintained files
    # inside data directories, placed only now - after the live
    # data carryover - so every apply refreshes them.
    for item in extras:
        if not item.get("after_data"):
            continue
        src = os.path.join(payload_dir, item["src"])
        dst = os.path.join(staging, item["dst"])
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dst), exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(src, dst)
    # No version marker in the staged tree: RECIPE-VERSION.txt
    # is written host-side by _save_state when this apply
    # completes (deanonymization audit R-03).

    # ---- seed (first install only; SQL-seeded recipes) --------
    # Installer-driven recipes (manifest "install") seed later,
    # via the install hook; their `seeded` is preserved from the
    # state record (the hook maintains it), never reset by an
    # update apply.
    seeded = bool(state.get("seeded"))
    if first_install and seed_sql_path is not None:
        staged_import = os.path.join(APPDATA, "db-import.sql.gz")
        if os.path.exists(staged_import):
            # A restore (or an earlier apply that died after
            # staging) already put an import there; it wins -
            # overwriting it could destroy restored data.
            print("onionhost-recipe: an import is already staged "
                  "at %s; leaving it in place" % staged_import)
            seeded = True
        else:
            if not onion:
                return fail("no onion address yet; cannot build "
                            "the seed")
            seed_sql = build_seed_sql(manifest, seed_sql_path,
                                      tree_root, onion, admin)
            blob = gzip.compress(seed_sql.encode("utf-8"))
            _atomic_write(staged_import, blob, mode=0o644)
            seeded = True
        # The staged administrator credentials have done their
        # one job. Shred the file so the plaintext password does
        # not linger on the host.
        _shred_file(ADMIN_ENV)

    # ---- overlays ----------------------------------------------
    _write_overlays(recipe_id)

    # The whole staged tree is given to the container's
    # www-data mapping BEFORE the swap (D1): this tool runs as
    # host root, and a host-root-owned docroot is "nobody"-
    # owned inside the container - PHP-FPM gets Permission
    # denied on every page whose mode is not world-readable
    # (the credentials-bearing config above all), and the app
    # init's normalise pass cannot repair unmapped owners.
    # The same chown on the rollback path (rollback_recipe)
    # covers trees staged by older appliers.
    _chown_tree(staging, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
    # The declared data directories get their app-writable
    # assertion (mapped group + setgid + owner ACL) before the
    # swap, so the tree that goes live already satisfies D8's
    # rule on every directory the application creates in.
    _own_data_dirs(staging, manifest)
    _own_www_root(staging)

    # ---- swap ---------------------------------------------------
    prev = os.path.join(APPDATA, ".www-recipe-prev")
    prev_version = state.get("version")
    if os.path.isdir(www) and os.listdir(www):
        shutil.rmtree(prev, ignore_errors=True)
        os.rename(www, prev)
    elif os.path.isdir(www):
        os.rmdir(www)
    os.rename(staging, www)
    _apply_mode_exceptions(recipe_id)

    _save_state(recipe_id, {
        "id": recipe_id,
        "version": version,
        "app_version": manifest["app"]["version"],
        "installed": True,
        "seeded": seeded,
        "prev_version": prev_version,
        "applied_at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ",
                                    time.gmtime()),
    })
    shutil.rmtree(workdir, ignore_errors=True)
    print("RECIPE_APPLY_OK id=%s version=%s first_install=%s "
          "seeded=%s" % (recipe_id, version,
                         "yes" if first_install else "no",
                         "yes" if seeded else "no"))
    return 0


def rollback_recipe(recipe_id):
    state = _load_state(recipe_id)
    manifest = _load_manifest(recipe_id)
    if manifest.get("runtime") == "native":
        # The native twin of the tree rollback: put the previous
        # binary back (the config and data were never swapped).
        native = os.path.join(APPDATA, "native")
        app = os.path.join(native, "app")
        prevb = os.path.join(native, ".app-prev")
        if not os.path.isfile(prevb):
            return fail("no previous binary kept for recipe %s"
                        % recipe_id)
        brokenb = os.path.join(native, ".app-broken")
        if os.path.isfile(app):
            os.replace(app, brokenb)
        os.replace(prevb, app)
        # A binary staged by an older applier may be host-root
        # owned; the restored binary gets the www-data mapping
        # like a fresh apply (D1, rollback path).
        os.chown(app, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
        # The root the binary lives in gets the same assertion
        # as a fresh apply (D7, rollback path).
        _own_native_root(native)
        _write_overlays(recipe_id)
        state["version"] = state.get("prev_version")
        state["prev_version"] = None
        state["applied_at"] = time.strftime(
            "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
        _save_state(recipe_id, state)
        print("RECIPE_ROLLBACK_OK id=%s version=%s (broken "
              "binary kept at %s)"
              % (recipe_id, state["version"], brokenb))
        return 0
    www = os.path.join(APPDATA, "www")
    prev = os.path.join(APPDATA, ".www-recipe-prev")
    if not os.path.isdir(prev):
        return fail("no previous tree kept for recipe %s"
                    % recipe_id)
    broken = os.path.join(APPDATA, ".www-recipe-broken")
    shutil.rmtree(broken, ignore_errors=True)
    if os.path.isdir(www):
        os.rename(www, broken)
    os.rename(prev, www)
    # The restored tree gets the www-data mapping too (D1,
    # rollback path): a tree staged before the applier learned
    # to chown - e.g. the previous generation of a site whose
    # update just failed - must not come back "nobody"-owned.
    _chown_tree(www, CONTAINER_WWW_UID, CONTAINER_WWW_GID)
    _own_data_dirs(www, manifest)
    _own_www_root(www)
    # Re-apply the installed payload's overlays (after a package
    # rollback the payload on disk is the previous version's).
    _write_overlays(recipe_id)
    state["version"] = state.get("prev_version")
    state["prev_version"] = None
    state["applied_at"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ",
                                        time.gmtime())
    _save_state(recipe_id, state)
    print("RECIPE_ROLLBACK_OK id=%s version=%s (broken tree kept "
          "at %s)" % (recipe_id, state["version"], broken))
    return 0


# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Install hook: drive the app's own installer over the edge
# socket (manifest "install": {"type": "http-form", ...}).
# General recipe capability - nothing in here is WordPress-
# specific beyond what the manifest declares.

class _UnixHTTPConnection(http.client.HTTPConnection):
    """HTTP over the website edge unix socket. The Host header
    carries the onion address, so the app renders its real
    URLs."""

    def __init__(self, sock_path, host, timeout=60):
        super().__init__(host, timeout=timeout)
        self._sock_path = sock_path

    def connect(self):
        self.sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX,
                                  socket.SOCK_STREAM)
        self.sock.settimeout(self.timeout)
        self.sock.connect(self._sock_path)


def _hook_request(method, path, host, fields=None, timeout=60):
    conn = _UnixHTTPConnection(EDGE_SOCK, host, timeout=timeout)
    body = None
    headers = {}
    if fields is not None:
        body = urllib.parse.urlencode(fields)
        headers["Content-Type"] = \
            "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"
    conn.request(method, path, body=body, headers=headers)
    resp = conn.getresponse()
    text = resp.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
    result = (resp.status, resp.getheader("Location"), text)
    conn.close()
    return result


def _hook_finish(recipe_id, manifest, state, via, onion):
    """Installation is verified (just now, or the app itself
    says it already was): remove the installer files from the
    served tree, mark the recipe seeded, and shred the staged
    administrator env - its one job is done. A front-page probe
    is reported as an advisory only: themes differ, the
    installer's own word is the verdict."""
    www = os.path.join(APPDATA, "www")
    for rel in manifest.get("install_files", []):
        try:
            os.unlink(os.path.join(www, rel))
        except OSError:
            pass
    # Files the installer just created (its users file above
    # all) get the recipe's exception modes NOW - the container
    # init's pass ran before they existed.
    _apply_mode_exceptions(recipe_id)
    state.update({
        "id": recipe_id,
        "version": manifest["version"],
        "app_version": manifest["app"]["version"],
        "installed": True,
        "seeded": True,
        "install_hook": via,
        "applied_at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ",
                                    time.gmtime()),
    })
    _save_state(recipe_id, state)
    _shred_file(ADMIN_ENV)
    spec = manifest.get("install") or {}
    marker = spec.get("home_marker")
    if marker:
        try:
            status, _, body = _hook_request("GET", "/", onion,
                                            timeout=30)
            print("onionhost-recipe: front page HTTP %s, "
                  "default-content marker %s (advisory)"
                  % (status, "seen" if marker in body
                     else "NOT seen"))
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
            print("onionhost-recipe: front page probe failed "
                  "(advisory): %s" % e)
    print("INSTALL_HOOK_OK id=%s via=%s" % (recipe_id, via))
    return 0


def install_hook(recipe_id):
    manifest = _load_manifest(recipe_id)
    spec = manifest.get("install")
    if not spec or not spec.get("type"):
        # Not an installer-driven recipe; publish calls this for
        # every recipe publish, so doing nothing is success.
        return 0
    if spec.get("type") != "http-form":
        return fail("recipe %s: unsupported install hook type %r"
                    % (recipe_id, spec.get("type")))
    state = _load_state(recipe_id)
    config_file = manifest.get("config_file") or "Settings.php"
    www = os.path.join(APPDATA, "www")
    if state.get("seeded") and os.path.isfile(
            os.path.join(www, config_file)):
        print("INSTALL_HOOK_OK id=%s via=already-seeded"
              % recipe_id)
        return 0
    try:
        onion = open(HS_HOSTNAME).read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return fail("install hook: no onion address yet")
    probe_path = spec["probe_path"]
    installed_marker = spec["installed_marker"]
    # Wait for the installer to answer usefully: the edge socket
    # existing only proves nginx is up; PHP and the database can
    # still be seconds behind it.
    ready = False
    deadline = time.time() + 180
    while time.time() < deadline:
        try:
            status, location, body = _hook_request(
                "GET", probe_path, onion, timeout=30)
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException):
            time.sleep(3)
            continue
        if installed_marker in body:
            return _hook_finish(recipe_id, manifest, state,
                                "already-installed", onion)
        if location and "setup-config" in location:
            return fail("install hook: the app has no config "
                        "file (apply did not run first?)")
        if status == 200 and spec["form_marker"] in body:
            ready = True
            break
        time.sleep(3)
    if not ready:
        return fail("install hook: the installer at %s did not "
                    "become ready" % probe_path)
    # Installing needs the owner's administrator details. If
    # they are not staged there is nothing honest to install
    # with - fail BEFORE posting anything.
    if not os.path.isfile(ADMIN_ENV):
        return fail("install hook: the administrator details "
                    "are not staged (recipe-setup); the site is "
                    "up but not installed - stage them and "
                    "publish again")
    env = _read_env_file(ADMIN_ENV)
    if env.get("RECIPE_ID") != recipe_id:
        return fail("install hook: staged administrator details "
                    "are for recipe %r, not %r"
                    % (env.get("RECIPE_ID"), recipe_id))
    user = env.get("RECIPE_ADMIN_USER", "").strip()
    password = env.get("RECIPE_ADMIN_PASSWORD", "")
    if not user or not password:
        return fail("install hook: staged administrator details "
                    "are incomplete")
    values = {
        "user": user,
        "password": password,
        "email": env.get("RECIPE_ADMIN_EMAIL")
                 or "admin@" + onion,
        "title": env.get("RECIPE_SITE_TITLE")
                 or spec.get("default_title", "My Website"),
        "onion": onion,
    }
    fields = {}
    for name, template in spec["fields"].items():
        value = template
        for key, val in values.items():
            value = value.replace("{%s}" % key, val)
        fields[name] = value
    try:
        status, location, body = _hook_request(
            "POST", spec["form_path"], onion, fields=fields,
            timeout=300)
    except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
        # The POST may still have completed server-side (a slow
        # install can outlive the socket read); one probe tells
        # the truth either way before this is called a failure.
        try:
            _, _, probe = _hook_request("GET", probe_path, onion,
                                        timeout=30)
            if installed_marker in probe:
                return _hook_finish(recipe_id, manifest, state,
                                    "installed", onion)
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException):
            pass
        return fail("install hook: installer POST failed: %s" % e)
    if spec["success_marker"] in body:
        return _hook_finish(recipe_id, manifest, state,
                            "installed", onion)
    if installed_marker in body:
        return _hook_finish(recipe_id, manifest, state,
                            "already-installed", onion)
    excerpt = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", body)
    excerpt = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", excerpt)[:300]
    return fail("install hook: the installer did not accept "
                "the details (HTTP %s): %s" % (status, excerpt))


def main():
    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        print("onionhost-recipe: must run as root", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if len(sys.argv) != 3 or sys.argv[1] not in (
            "apply", "rollback", "status", "install-hook",
            "await-first-boot", "is-installed"):
        print("usage: onionhost-recipe "
              "apply|rollback|status|install-hook|"
              "await-first-boot|is-installed <recipe-id>",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    cmd, recipe_id = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*", recipe_id):
        print("onionhost-recipe: bad recipe id %r" % recipe_id,
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        if cmd == "is-installed":
            # A26 gate query (the recipe package's postinst asks
            # this before applying): exit 0 iff the state record
            # carries a completed apply - see _app_installed.
            state = _load_state(recipe_id)
            if _app_installed(recipe_id):
                print("recipe %s: installed (version %s)"
                      % (recipe_id, state.get("version")))
                return 0
            print("recipe %s: not installed" % recipe_id)
            return 1
        if cmd == "apply":
            return apply_recipe(recipe_id)
        if cmd == "rollback":
            return rollback_recipe(recipe_id)
        if cmd == "install-hook":
            return install_hook(recipe_id)
        if cmd == "await-first-boot":
            return await_first_boot(recipe_id)
        state = _load_state(recipe_id)
        manifest = _load_manifest(recipe_id)
        print("recipe %s: payload version %s; applied: %s"
              % (recipe_id, manifest["version"],
                 json.dumps(state, sort_keys=True) if state
                 else "never"))
        return 0
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, zipfile.BadZipFile,
            tarfile.TarError, EOFError) as e:
        return fail(str(e))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
